      "section_title": "Taking a Straw Poll",
      "chapter_opener": true,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "b1",
          "kind": "exposition",
          "markdown": "The chair states the question clearly and makes sure the room understands what is being asked. If needed, the chair restates the question in neutral terms before calling for the poll.\n\nThe chair then counts the room, recording the number of hands in each column. WG21 uses five columns: *strongly favor* (SF), *favor* (F), *neutral* (N), *against* (A), and *strongly against* (SA). The results are reported back to the room as five numbers, for example `9 14 5 2 1`.[^1] Read the [full poll guidance](https://isocpp.org/std/meetings-and-participation) before your first meeting."
        },
        {
          "id": "b2",
          "kind": "exposition",
          "markdown": "| SF | F | N | A | SA |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 9 | 14 | 5 | 2 | 1 |"
        },
        {
          "id": "b3",
          "kind": "exposition",
          "markdown": "### Before you call the poll\n\nWork through these steps in order:\n\n1. Read the abstract\n2. Find the motivating example\n3. Check the proposed wording\n4. Write down one question\n\nA good proposal keeps four things in view:\n\n- *Problem:* What precisely is difficult, ambiguous, or missing?\n- *Motivation:* Who runs into this, and what does it cost them?\n- *Design:* What is the smallest language change that removes the problem?\n- *Wording:* How would you explain the change to a working C++ programmer?"
        },
        {
          "id": "b4",
          "kind": "exposition",
          "markdown": "> Consensus is more than a majority. It asks whether the whole delegation can live with the outcome."
        },
